            <ArticleTitle>Advances in high-density lipoprotein physiology: surprises, overturns, and promises.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E1-E14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1152/ajpendo.00429.2015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Emerging evidence strongly supports that changes in the HDL metabolic pathway, which result in changes in HDL proteome and function, appear to have a causative impact on a number of metabolic disorders. Here, we provide a critical review of the most recent and novel findings correlating HDL properties and functionality with various pathophysiological processes and disease states, such as obesity, type 2 diabetes mellitus, nonalcoholic fatty liver disease, inflammation and sepsis, bone and obstructive pulmonary diseases, and brain disorders.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2016 the American Physiological Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
